7. ACCOUNTING SERVICE
=====================

Create a centralized Accounting Service.

The exact class/file structure should follow the existing architecture, but the system should have a reusable service responsible for accounting operations.

The service should support operations similar to:

Create Journal
Validate Journal
Approve Journal
Post Journal
Reverse Journal
Get Ledger
Get Account Balance
Get Trial Balance

The Accounting Service must be the ONLY recommended entry point for creating accounting entries from application code.

Future modules should be able to call something conceptually similar to:

AccountingService::createJournal(...)

Do not allow each module to directly insert journal lines into the database.

The Accounting Service should:

1. Validate the transaction.
2. Validate accounts.
3. Validate debit/credit amounts.
4. Validate the journal balance.
5. Create the journal header.
6. Create journal lines.
7. Save everything inside a database transaction.
8. Roll back everything if any operation fails.

==================================================
8. DATABASE TRANSACTIONS
========================

Use MySQL database transactions for accounting operations.

Example:

BEGIN TRANSACTION

Create Journal Header

Create Journal Lines

Validate Debit = Credit

Commit

If anything fails:

ROLLBACK

Never allow partially created journals.

Example failure:

Journal Header created successfully.

Line 1 created.

Line 2 fails.

The entire transaction must be rolled back.

The system must not leave an incomplete journal.

==================================================
9. POSTING ENGINE
=================

Create a proper posting process.

When a journal is POSTED:

1. Validate the journal.
2. Confirm total debit = total credit.
3. Confirm all accounts are active.
4. Confirm all required fields.
5. Create/update General Ledger records.
6. Mark journal as POSTED.
7. Record Posted By.
8. Record Posted At.
9. Create Audit Log.

Only POSTED journals should appear in the official General Ledger.

Do not duplicate financial amounts unnecessarily.

The General Ledger should have a clear relationship with the original journal.

Each ledger entry should be traceable back to:

Journal
→ Journal Lines
→ Source Transaction

